      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ec1ca0e0-7cf8-5142-848d-f627a3fff436",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Ships: HMS Warspite, Queen Elizabeth-class battleships, Iron Duke-class, König-class, Bayern-class\n- Organizations: Royal Navy\n- Topics: Battleship design, naval service, World War I, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- The Queen Elizabeth-class battleships were a class of five Royal Navy super-dreadnoughts commissioned in 1915 and 1916.\n- The Queen Elizabeth-class battleships had greater firepower, protection, and speed than the Royal Navy's earlier Iron Duke-class battleships and the earlier German König-class battleships.\n- The German Bayern-class battleships were competitive with the Queen Elizabeth class, but they were 2 knots slower.\n- HMS Warspite served for thirty years across both World War I and World War II.\n- HMS Warspite operated in the Atlantic, Indian, Arctic, and Pacific Oceans during her career.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "11222d3f-4cf8-5c5f-b99d-516b188a670b",
        "text": "Page: HMS Warspite (03). Sentence: Her thirty-year career covered both world wars and took her across the Atlantic, Indian, Arctic and Pacific Oceans.\nPage: Queen Elizabeth-class battleship. Sentence: The \"Queen Elizabeth\"-class battleships were a class of five super-dreadnoughts of the Royal Navy commissioned in 1915–16.\nPage: Queen Elizabeth-class battleship. Sentence: These battleships were superior in firepower, protection and speed to their Royal Navy predecessors of the \"Iron Duke\" class as well as preceding German classes such as the \"König\" class , although the corresponding \"Bayern\"-class ships were competitive except for being 2 kn slower.",
